# Backend Laravel

Apply when editing backend PHP/Laravel files. Prefer existing patterns in `backend/` over inventing new structure. Adapt paths if the app uses a standard Laravel layout (`app/`, `routes/`, `database/`).

## Structure

```text
backend/
├── app/
│   ├── Http/Controllers/
│   ├── Http/Requests/
│   ├── Http/Resources/
│   ├── Models/
│   ├── Services/
│   └── Repositories/   # optional; use if already established
├── routes/
├── database/migrations/
└── tests/
```

## Layering

- Controllers: transport, auth middleware, response mapping only.
- Form Requests: validation and authorization at the boundary.
- Services: business logic and workflows.
- Models/Eloquent (or repositories): persistence.
- API Resources: response shaping.
- Do not put business logic or raw query building in controllers.

## Conventions

- Prefer dependency injection; avoid `new` for app services inside controllers.
- Use Form Requests instead of inline `$request->validate()` when validation is non-trivial.
- Prefer Eloquent relationships and query scopes; avoid N+1 (eager load).
- Schema changes go through migrations only; do not edit production schema ad hoc.
- Use policies/gates for authorization; reuse existing middleware.
- Prefer queued jobs for slow/side-effect work (mail, webhooks, heavy processing).
- Do not invent public API or schema changes without an explicit request.
- Keep feature code cohesive; follow existing naming and folder layout.

## Example

```php
// ❌ BAD — validation + DB + business rules in controller
public function store(Request $request)
{
    $data = $request->validate(['email' => 'required|email']);
    $user = User::create($data);
    Mail::to($user)->send(new WelcomeMail($user));
    return response()->json($user);
}

// ✅ GOOD — Form Request + service + resource
public function store(StoreUserRequest $request, UserService $users)
{
    $user = $users->create($request->validated());
    return new UserResource($user);
}
```
